genParse EMITS litTo() AND NOTHING IMPLEMENTS IT

Read the emission printed above. That is genParse's output for `break`, and it
is correct -- rule-ladder rung two landed the ZERO MEANS SELF convention that
makes `t0 = rule` bind to the rule node itself. The odometer counts break GREEN.

The last line of the body is

    return leaveRule(rule,into,label,from, litTo(t0,label,"break","break") );

and `litTo` DOES NOT EXIST.

HOW IT WAS FOUND, and it is worth knowing that no amount of reading would have
found it. The body was spliced into genParse.rtn beside the parseScaf family
(the existing verbatim-emission precedent), retok'd, and the BUILD failed:

    GroupRules.mm:11123:42: error: use of undeclared identifier 'litTo'

tok itself had already said so, in the trailing block it prints and nobody
reads:

    /*  Warning: the following methods were referenced but not declared
        ...
        litTo(GroupItem*,GroupItem*,char*,char*)
        ...  */

⚠ SO COMPILE-GREEN ON THE ODOMETER DOES NOT MEAN BUILDABLE. The odometer asks
"can this rule be PLANNED AND EMITTED", and break can. Whether the emitted text
REFERS TO ANYTHING is a different question and the odometer does not ask it.
This is probeNameResolve's finding one level up: that probe showed the scaffold
count accepts calls to names that are not rules, so compile is a parse check.
This shows the odometer accepts emissions calling helpers that do not exist.
Both instruments are honest about what they measure; neither measures capability.

WHAT IS KNOWN, and it is unusually clean because the charter predicted it

`lit` EXISTS -- RuleStuff.twk:549, declared in groups.ext:716, used by the
shipped parseScaf and parseScaf2. It matches an unlabelled literal.
`litTo` is its LABELLED twin: match the literal AND attach it to the frame's
label under a named slot. break emits the labelled form because its rStuff does
not carry noLabel.

⚠ AND docs/genParseLadder.md ALREADY SAYS SO. The charter's own ladder:

    | 1 | single-literal sequence | ... | lit("x")          | no |
    | 2 | two-literal sequence    | ... | lit("{") && ...   | no |
    | 3 | labelled literal        | litTo, slot naming | litTo(label,slot,"x") | no |

Rungs 1 and 2 are built. RUNG 3 IS NOT, and break sits on it. This is not a
surprise defect -- it is a documented, ungated, unbuilt rung that the rule
ladder walked into from the other direction.

NEXT: OPEN

The SHAPE is obvious and the SEMANTICS are not, which is why this is OPEN rather
than a remedy: `lit` returns a bool after advancing atRuleMark, and the labelled
form additionally has to build the slot and attach it to `label`. What it
attaches, under what tag, and what happens on a failed match after a partial
attach are naming decisions with a parse-tree shape behind them -- and the
whole point of the odometer's per-rule verdict is that a wrong answer here would
be invisible in the count.

